The feature names are anonymized, so I can't tie any index to a known census variable. These scores are close to uniform rather than informed rankings.

The one small adjustment is a guess. With 63 columns, this is probably a one-hot encoding of the standard census dataset. Numeric columns such as age, years of education, capital gain and hours worked are often placed first. If so, they include some of the strongest predictors, so indices 0–5 get slightly higher scores. Everything else gets a neutral 0.4. Treat this as a weak prior, not evidence.

